// FUROR PETABYTORUM -- "the madness of petabytes": the seed module of PetaByte
// Madness. One job: turn (world, chunk index) into the 64-bit seed a chunk is
// grown from, and back. Pure arithmetic: no poscit, no Mundus, no initium, no
// allocation, no clock -- the same module is the same function on the VR4300
// and on x86-64.
//
//   semina_furore(orbis, index)    -> semen     sow: the seed of chunk `index`
//   desemina_furore(semen, orbis)  -> index     un-sow: which chunk is this seed
//   misce_furore(z)                -> u64       the mixer under both
//   demisce_furore(z)              -> u64       its inverse
//
// and the same pair addressed by chunk COORDINATES, which is how a game on a
// plane finds a chunk:
//
//   semina_plano(orbis, x, y)      -> semen     sow the chunk at (x, y)
//   desemina_plano(semen, orbis, xy) -> u8      xy[0], xy[1] = the (x, y) of this seed
//
// THE NAMES. `semina` is the first-conjugation imperative of *semino*, to sow
// (the -a realisation of §3.4's `-e` category; `plica` and `saluta` are the same
// shape), and `misce` of *misceo*, to mix. `de-` is §3.5's reversal: the base's
// return type becomes the FIRST parameter, so `desemina_furore` takes the seed
// before the world -- which is why its parameters are in that order and not
// `semina`'s. After the `_` is §3.1's one qualifier, an ablative, unchecked:
// `furore`, "by madness". *Furor* is the module's name and, with *petabyta*,
// the game's: Furor Petabytorum.
// `[OPEN]` §3.3's morpheme table is fourteen illustrative roots and the lexicon
// pass is not enabled, so `semin-` and `misc-` are in no table: when
// `lexicon.norma` exists they enter it through §3.9, and this is not that.
//
// WHAT IT GUARANTEES, and how each is held.
//
//   Distinct chunks have distinct seeds. `semina_furore` is
//   `misce_furore(orbis + index * GAMMA)`, and every step is a bijection of u64:
//   GAMMA is odd, so index -> index * GAMMA is invertible mod 2^64, and the
//   mixer is two xorshift-rights and two odd multiplies, each invertible. So for
//   one `orbis` the 2^64 indices get 2^64 different seeds, by construction. That
//   is what a petabyte of distinct chunks needs: 10^15 / 4,096 is exactly
//   244,140,625,000 chunks, and drawing that many seeds AT RANDOM from 2^64
//   would collide about 1,600 times.
//   `desemina_furore` is the proof run: it computes the inverse, so
//   `desemina_furore(semina_furore(o, i), o) == i` for every o and i is a
//   property tests/programs/dungeon/ checks in the language, not only in the
//   Python oracle.
//
//   One seed is not quite one chunk. xorshift64, which grows a chunk from its
//   seed, has 0 as its fixed point, so a seed of 0 is replaced by GAMMA
//   (dungeon.exsc). Exactly one index per world mixes to 0, and that chunk
//   repeats the layout of the one index whose seed is GAMMA.
//   `desemina_furore(0, orbis)` names the first and `desemina_furore(GAMMA,
//   orbis)` the second. For orbis 0 they are 0 and 0x32980CC117E5229F: the
//   second is ~3.6 * 10^18, so inside the first petabyte (indices below
//   244,140,625,000) no two chunks of world 0 share a layout. For a world
//   chosen at random the chance that either index falls in the first petabyte
//   is about 10^-8 each.
//
//   The mixer is splitmix64's finaliser: `misce_furore(GAMMA)` is its published
//   first output for seed 0, 0xE220A8397B1DCDAF, which probatio.exsc checks.
//
// WHY THE MULTIPLY IS HAND-ROLLED. §5.4 leaves `*%` `[OPEN]`. Measured on the
// mips64 row (clang -O2 -march=mips3 n32, qemu -one-insn-per-tb), one
// `semina_furore` is 91 instructions against 2,483,730 for the chunk it seeds,
// so a native wrapping multiply would save a few dozen instructions per chunk
// and no compiler work is justified by it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// a * b mod 2^64 without `*%` -- examples/metronomus/metronomus.exsc,
// `multiplica_modulo`. The low product is below 2^64; each cross product is
// reduced mod 2^32 before it is added, so their sum is below 2^33 and
// `sursum 32` drops what belongs above bit 63. The final add wraps on purpose.
functio multiplica_modulo(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {
    firma al = (a sursum 32) deorsum 32;
    firma ah = a deorsum 32;
    firma bl = (b sursum 32) deorsum 32;
    firma bh = b deorsum 32;
    firma x = (((ah * bl) sursum 32) deorsum 32) + (((al * bh) sursum 32) deorsum 32);
    redde (al * bl) +% (x sursum 32);
}

// Inverts y = x ^ (x >> k). The top k bits of y are x's, so r = y starts right
// in its top k bits, and each round r = y ^ (r >> k) makes k more right: after n
// rounds the top (n + 1) * k are. k is 27, 30 or 31 here, so two rounds cover all
// 64. (A first draft ran three, the oracle's ceil(64 / k), which counts r = y as
// a round; the third was redundant and mutation testing found it -- dropping it
// to two survived, and to one does not.)
functio inverte_dextrorsum(z: u64, k: u64) -> u64 {
    mutabilis r: u64 = z;
    per i in 0..2 {
        r = z aut (r deorsum k);
    }
    redde r;
}

// The splitmix64 finaliser: a bijection of u64.
publica functio misce_furore(z: u64) -> u64 {
    firma a: u64 = z aut (z deorsum 30);
    firma b: u64 = multiplica_modulo(a, 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9);
    firma c: u64 = b aut (b deorsum 27);
    firma d: u64 = multiplica_modulo(c, 0x94D049BB133111EB);
    redde d aut (d deorsum 31);
}

// Its inverse: undo each step in the opposite order, multiplying by the
// modular inverse of each odd constant (0x319642B2D24D8EC3 for the second,
// 0x96DE1B173F119089 for the first -- checked against the constants by the
// oracle's `unmix64`, and by the round trip in probatio.exsc).
publica functio demisce_furore(z: u64) -> u64 {
    firma d: u64 = inverte_dextrorsum(z, 31);
    firma c: u64 = multiplica_modulo(d, 0x319642B2D24D8EC3);
    firma b: u64 = inverte_dextrorsum(c, 27);
    firma a: u64 = multiplica_modulo(b, 0x96DE1B173F119089);
    redde inverte_dextrorsum(a, 30);
}

// The seed of chunk `index` in world `orbis`. world + index * GAMMA, mixed.
publica functio semina_furore(orbis: u64, index: u64) -> u64 {
    firma g: u64 = 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15;
    redde misce_furore(orbis +% multiplica_modulo(index, g));
}

// The chunk of world `orbis` whose seed is `semen`: semina_furore's inverse.
// `de-` puts the base's return type first (§3.5), hence (semen, orbis).
publica functio desemina_furore(semen: u64, orbis: u64) -> u64 {
    firma g_inversus: u64 = 0xF1DE83E19937733D;
    redde multiplica_modulo(demisce_furore(semen) -% orbis, g_inversus);
}

// ---- the plane --------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A game addresses a chunk by where it is, not by a counter. (x, y) are two
// u32s and the chunk index is `(y << 32) | x`: a bijection of u32 x u32 onto u64,
// so `semina_furore`'s guarantee carries over whole -- every one of the 2^64
// chunks of a plane, 4,294,967,296 on a side, has its own seed. (At 4,096 bytes a
// chunk, that plane is 7.5 * 10^22 bytes; the first petabyte, 244,140,625,000
// chunks, is NOT a square -- it has no integer square root, 494,105^2 falling
// short and 494,106^2 over -- but 2^19 = 524,288 on a side is exactly one
// pebibyte, 2^50 bytes, and probatio.exsc checks that arithmetic.)
//
// THE COORDINATES ARE UNSIGNED, and a world centred on the origin wants signed
// ones. The cast is the answer and it is a bijection: an i32 chunk coordinate
// `c` is passed as `c sicut u32` (§5.4: an equal-width sign change reinterprets
// the bits, never traps), so (-1, 0) is (0xFFFFFFFF, 0) and every signed
// coordinate has its own seed. Taking u32 here and not i32 keeps the C ABI
// natural: the C backend holds an iN sign-extended in its uint64_t carrier, and
// a C caller that passes a zero-extended negative number would be handing it a
// non-canonical value.

// y in the high word, x in the low.
functio compone_plano(x: u32, y: u32) -> u64 {
    redde ((y sicut u64) sursum 32) sive (x sicut u64);
}

// The seed of the chunk at (x, y) in world `orbis`.
publica functio semina_plano(orbis: u64, x: u32, y: u32) -> u64 {
    redde semina_furore(orbis, compone_plano(x, y));
}

// The chunk of world `orbis` whose seed is `semen`, as xy[0] = x, xy[1] = y.
// Output through a mutable borrow (ADR 0016): the language has no tuples.
publica functio desemina_plano(semen: u64, orbis: u64, xy: &mutabilis acies<u32, 2>) -> u8 {
    firma index: u64 = desemina_furore(semen, orbis);
    (*xy)[0] = index sicut u32;
    (*xy)[1] = (index deorsum 32) sicut u32;
    redde 0;
}
